WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.200
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Matthew Tai, known online as Lao Wai

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.430
86, or Si Milk, who's an American blogger
well known for living in China.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.190
Matthew, so great having you on.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:14.190
I really appreciate it.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:15.550
Speaker 4: Thank you so much.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:16.550
It's an honor.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.600
Speaker 1: So, I mean, I guess first, just
to kind of orient our audience, how did you

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.360
end up living in China for the period that
you did?

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.000
Speaker 4: Yeah, I'll give you the CliffsNotes
version.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.630
But basically, I was bored out of college.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:32.119
I didn't really want to settle down and get
a job immediately.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:33.960
I had just backpacked through Europe.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.559
I was like, Why not go travel somewhere else?

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:38.600
China wasn't even on my list.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.190
I was like, Why not do some sort of gap year?

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.649
Maybe teach, you know, teach English abroad
for a year and then come back home and then

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:45.859
get a job.

00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:49.829
So I initially thought, you know, Taiwan sounds
pretty cool.

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:52.480
Interested in maybe learning Mandarin Chinese
might be useful in the future.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:58.289
So I put up my resume on some Taiwanese job
sites and it just wasn't hiring seasoned at

00:00:58.289 --> 00:00:59.949
the time.

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:04.500
But I guess some mainland Chinese companies
also poach like potential employees on those

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.870
sites as well.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:11.550
Because I got a phone call from this like
tiny, tiny little school in Guangdong in Hawaii,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.960
who actually the picture behind me is the
city behind me called me up and said, hey,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.230
do you want to do a year here instead?

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:21.140
And I you know, I didn't really consider I
was like, you know, China is a pretty it seems

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.490
like a pretty crazy place.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:27.140
But at the same time, why not go on another
adventure and try it out and, you know, sold

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.140
all my stuff.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.630
I was just a young, you know, 22 year old
kid and hopped on a plane and went over there.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:37.280
Speaker 1: And I have a number of viewers
who have called me sometimes when we'll open

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.740
up our phone lines and they say that they
are in China teaching English.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.290
What other things do Americans sometimes do
in China when they're there?

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.350
Speaker 4: Sure.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.350
Yeah.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:48.930
So I didn't continue teaching English the
entire time.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.040
It's kind of just your it's a way you get
you when you get off the plane.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.040
It's what you do.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.040
Right.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.840
But a lot of people, you know, they'll oversee
factories, they'll do consultation for like,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.480
what's it called, like quality control.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:07.340
They'll do help with immigration visas and
stuff for people that want to move abroad.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.670
There's people that'll start their own bars.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:12.879
You know, it's like a it's not an open market,
but it's a huge market.

00:02:12.879 --> 00:02:17.299
So a lot of people, especially during China's
golden period, which I call like kind of like

00:02:17.299 --> 00:02:25.129
when I was there 28 up until I mean, I left
around 2018, but up until like 2014 was like

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:30.319
a golden period for people to go there and
try out a new market, go try a new product,

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:34.239
try to get Chinese people interested in something
that they can find at home that is not already

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:35.299
popular in China.

00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:39.260
Speaker 1: So there's how do you even broach
this next part?

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:47.090
I mean, there's no shortage of reporting about
an authoritarian status quo in China to different

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.530
degrees and affecting different people.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:55.359
We have the weaker population specifically
and we've done interviews about that.

00:02:55.359 --> 00:03:00.170
Then there's people like you who are Americans
who may be there on a temporary basis, and

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:04.920
your circumstances presumably would be different
than someone who is from China and living

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.260
in China just to get us into this.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:13.109
Can you give us a general overview of what's
accurate about the reporting that we hear

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:17.400
about the Chinese government and what maybe
is is less accurate, generally speaking?

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.400
Speaker 4: Sure.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:24.359
So when I was initially there, I mean, I didn't
set out to cover any news or influence anything

00:03:24.359 --> 00:03:29.250
about China other than the fact that I wanted
to put out videos to show people that it's

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.250
not the boogeyman.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.099
Like, people are like, what are you doing
over there?

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:36.459
Why did you move to such a crazy communist,
authoritarian country?

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:39.420
And I just wanted to put videos out there
to be like, listen, it's not it's not as bad

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:40.420
as what you think.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.060
Or maybe the things that you're thinking of
are not actually true, at least in my life,

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:45.870
from my perspective or my Chinese friends.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:46.870
Right?

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.779
So that's what I initially did.

00:03:48.779 --> 00:03:52.799
And I actually moved into as my channel and
got bigger and stuff.

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:57.079
And I, you know, my business partner, people
might know him out there.

00:03:57.079 --> 00:04:01.480
Winston, he is the first China blogger, the
first guy to actually get on a VPN and put

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:02.769
out videos from China.

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:06.550
I happened to be the second we met each other
and we started doing TV shows.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:08.819
We started doing travel documentaries.

00:04:08.819 --> 00:04:14.930
And again, our goal was we set up a small
motorcycle business where we built our own

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:15.930
custom motorcycle.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.680
So we rode through the country and we rode
we didn't go through the places like that.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.169
You know, people normally see Shanghai, Beijing.

00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:23.810
We went through the rural countryside.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:28.150
We went through the small towns, the third
tier cities, all these places that don't get

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:29.169
reporting.

00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:34.699
So we wanted to show a human face of China,
and we didn't set out to do that from the

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:38.490
lens of like, Oh, this is so unfair the way
it's portrayed.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:41.210
We just wanted to show reality, right?

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:46.169
So from that perspective, we developed our
own insight onto what was actually happening

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:47.560
in the country.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.440
And as we traveled more and the more people
and relationships we developed, the more we

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.020
realized that.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:59.060
Unfortunately, a lot of stuff that people
talk about in regards to China are more of

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:00.720
a macro scale.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.669
But China has a lot of issues and a lot of
them, I'm going to say the majority of them

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:08.740
do stem from the Chinese government.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:15.479
And when we would go to some of these villages
and find out that maybe an official, for example,

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:21.940
a local official went unpunished after raping
a 14 year old girl and then having her murdered,

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:23.620
we would be shocked to hear this.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:27.310
And it's weird because the people in this
village, they normally probably wouldn't talk

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:28.470
about that to anyone else.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:32.120
But because we were foreigners that showed
up, there were some sort of like trust or

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:36.789
understanding that that we would understand
would be would kind of get their perspective

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:40.170
and we wouldn't stick around long enough to
face any repercussion.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:45.050
And the more people like that we met, the
more beholden we felt to tell the story of

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:46.760
actually what's happening.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:51.919
When we were in some of these far flung areas,
we would find out that the People's Liberation

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:56.720
Army of China would go and harass families
and harass tribes like Mongolian tribes and

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.919
things like this for money to just keep them
down.

00:06:00.919 --> 00:06:04.320
Their languages were being banned out of schools.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.240
The people would tell the stories about how
they felt trapped because they're an ethnic

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:09.240
minority.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.259
And China doesn't allow them mobility or it
doesn't allow them to do really anything,

00:06:13.259 --> 00:06:17.229
especially upwards mobility, owning their
own business, making money.

00:06:17.229 --> 00:06:23.569
And we found out that there's actually a lot
more than what Western media is portraying.

00:06:23.569 --> 00:06:28.050
I think the Western media portrays a lot of,
you know, financial based things, or we'll

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.840
focus on one specific issue, which is great.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:35.240
I mean, like I absolutely focus on the weaker
issue.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.240
It's a huge thing.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.520
I personally know weaker people that have
gone through this.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:43.990
I'm in personal contact with people that have
lost their families to the genocide in western

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:48.889
China, but also like the stories of the inner
Mongolian people, the stories of the ethnic

00:06:48.889 --> 00:06:50.610
minorities in the South.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:56.210
I became very dedicated to kind of compiling
these stories in my head while I was there,

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:00.270
knowing I couldn't report from China at the
time and then saving them for when I ultimately

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:01.280
had to leave China.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.259
Speaker 1: So let's dig into a couple of things
you said.

00:07:03.259 --> 00:07:07.560
You said in some of the, you know, third tier
cities, you wouldn't stick around long enough

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.440
that there would be repercussions.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.800
And then you just said some of the stories
you knew you couldn't report on while you

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:14.800
were there.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:15.800
Why?

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:16.800
Why not?

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:17.800
Why did you have to leave the cities?

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:19.169
What would the repercussions be?

00:07:19.169 --> 00:07:20.949
What would happen if you tried to report on
them?

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:25.360
Speaker 4: So what happened was when we we
would have the nicest of intentions.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.930
Let's say we show up at a, you know, a third,
fourth, fifth tier city somewhere down in

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:30.930
Guangdong Province.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.330
Let's just pick a random province in the south.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:36.919
And what would happen is we would you know,
of course, you show up, you're a foreigner

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:39.280
from the middle of nowhere, like you're in
the middle of nowhere.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:40.280
And people crowd around you.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:41.280
They want to talk to you.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.009
They want to take pictures, the all that kind
of stuff, that's normal.

00:07:44.009 --> 00:07:49.310
But when you sit down with people and you
start sharing a meal, when you start talking

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.349
about things and they, you know, you're not
inciting these conversations.

00:07:52.349 --> 00:07:55.669
They're they're bringing these things up and
they start talking about what life is like

00:07:55.669 --> 00:08:00.389
there, how they haven't received any of the
poverty alleviation type funds, how their

00:08:00.389 --> 00:08:04.389
their town is going to be flooded by a dam
that the government's putting in, even though

00:08:04.389 --> 00:08:06.440
that nobody wants it there.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.719
Those conversations perked the wrong ears.

00:08:08.719 --> 00:08:12.499
So like people will be in the background,
people will be around there that have to report

00:08:12.499 --> 00:08:14.849
to local Communist Party officials.

00:08:14.849 --> 00:08:19.069
And what will happen is they'll find out,
you know, through the grapevine quite quickly

00:08:19.069 --> 00:08:22.900
that there's foreigners there and that they're
not supposed to be there because there are

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:26.699
entire areas in China where foreigners are
not even supposed to be.

00:08:26.699 --> 00:08:27.861
And you don't hear about this.

00:08:27.861 --> 00:08:30.270
This is again, you wouldn't see this on reporting.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:31.639
You wouldn't read about this anywhere.

00:08:31.639 --> 00:08:36.019
But there are whole areas that are basically
blacklisted off because the Chinese government

00:08:36.019 --> 00:08:38.039
doesn't want another Xinjiang story to break.

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:40.409
They don't want another Tibet story to break.

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:44.670
They don't want people talking about issues
that are caused by the government leaking

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:47.490
out to potential journalists, even though
we weren't.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:53.260
And so you would have a situation where you'd
be in an area and then you'd kind of get this

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:57.310
inkling, this feeling that, number one, the
hotels won't even let you stay.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:58.310
Stay there.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:08:59.870
They're like, no, we can't register a foreigner.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.630
You know, whether they got tipped off by someone
or not is a different story, but they just

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:03.630
won't even register you.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:05.610
So you could stay there so you're forced to
leave anyway.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.880
But when you get the feeling that people are
starting to like make phone calls and then

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:13.700
you start to see police officers and things
like this, you know, it's time to leave.

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:16.930
Because the times that we did stick around,
we've been told by police officers that you

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:20.180
actually have to get on your bike and just
get the hell out of there.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:26.149
And what we noticed sometimes is when we would
be in these areas later on, we would see actual

00:09:26.149 --> 00:09:31.310
police officers sometimes in notepads or their
phones at certain kind of designated points

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:34.860
on the road from the next from where we just
left to the next city.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.530
So we'd be monitored the entire time.

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:42.380
And you realize that, you know, especially
when you travel around rural China, you realize

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:47.010
that it's very much akin to the security apparatus
that maybe like a place like North Korea would

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:48.010
have.

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:49.010
It's just not is in your face.

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:50.300
It has more money.

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.300
Right.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:52.320
So you're minded the entire time.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.200
And ultimately we found out.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:55.209
Really how bad it can get.

00:09:55.209 --> 00:10:00.339
When we were up in Inner Mongolia and our
hotel room was just full of SWAT team people

00:10:00.339 --> 00:10:03.200
with body cameras and automatic weapons.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.040
That's when you realize that I'm here trying
to film some horses and talk to some local

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:09.080
people about their way of life.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:13.180
And then there's a SWAT team and detectives
in my hotel room interrogating me about my

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:14.970
every movement in the entire country.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:21.010
Speaker 1: Now, when something like that happened,
after they're done interrogating you, do they

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:23.240
leave and then you just kind of finish what
you were doing?

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:28.570
Speaker 4: No, that's that that's the worst
part, is that there's a system of I don't

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:34.560
know, I think they want to instill some sort
of paranoia, because what they'll do is I'll

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.940
use the example in the hotel room of the SWAT
team.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:40.420
What they'll do is be like, okay, we didn't
really get what we wanted.

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:44.880
You know, they they list off where we work,
what our contracts were, where we shop 2000

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:45.990
miles away, by the way.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:50.000
And they know everything about you and they
want to get in your head because later on

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.440
they'll continue to harass you.

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:54.720
So you think everything's fine, you'll leave
super early the next morning to go on to your

00:10:54.720 --> 00:11:00.180
next destination, and then you get stopped
by the army like that, that later that day,

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.970
early in the morning, we had stopped by the
PLA, the People's Liberation Army, who made

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:09.300
us take apart all of our equipment, check
everything, take photos, told us not to smile

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:12.660
in the photo, stand in front of everything,
and they would leave.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:16.010
And then immediately they would come back
and do the exact same thing over and over

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.820
again until you just get out of their jurisdiction.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:19.820
Right.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:23.510
At the same time, after your trip is over,
the police still come to your house.

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:25.029
They come to visit where you are.

00:11:25.029 --> 00:11:26.030
They come to interrogate you.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:27.030
Why?

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:28.030
Why do you own a drone?

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:29.080
Why are you doing this right?

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:31.310
And it's this constant kind of harassment.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:37.220
And it's it's kind of the the moment you realize
that, hey, I'm not welcome here with a camera.

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:40.970
They don't want people they don't want foreigners
walking around with cameras, talking to people.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:46.050
And so that kind of false security you built
up over ten years.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:48.190
You know, you watch that shift with the current
regime.

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:51.949
By the way, this this really came to fruition
maybe around 2015, and it started getting

00:11:51.949 --> 00:11:53.320
really bad.

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:59.509
But you watch this happen and it breaks your
heart and it also makes you feel like what

00:11:59.509 --> 00:12:04.110
if if the Chinese government is trying to
hide this stuff so badly, there is so there's

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:07.000
so much under the surface that people don't
actually know about.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.920
And that's what I feel beholden.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:13.870
You know, I feel beholden to the people to
talk about today because I, I get thousands,

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:17.230
thousands of Chinese people reaching out,
telling me, please keep your voice up.

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:20.790
Please keep talking about this kind of stuff
because we can't talk about that stuff.

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:22.120
Speaker 1: Right.

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:28.029
The the social credit system, I've read quite
a bit about it specifically in the context

00:12:28.029 --> 00:12:32.810
of the wiggers, and we interviewed Jeffrey
Kane about his book and he writes specifically

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:37.990
about how how it's sometimes used to restrict
the movement of the weaker people and in other

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:38.990
ways.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:42.630
Can you talk a little more generally just
about how for the average person living in

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:46.570
China and maybe there is no such thing, maybe
it's very different urban versus rural or

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:48.010
whatever the case may be.

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.029
How does that system affect the average person?

00:12:50.029 --> 00:12:55.770
Speaker 4: Yeah, so the social credit system
is I've done a huge basically a documentary

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:57.839
series on the whole thing.

00:12:57.839 --> 00:13:01.410
What it is, is it's more of a dynamic thing
that's being tested out.

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:05.570
So I think a lot of people I wanted to go
out there and dispel this myth that it's some

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:09.560
sort of number that hangs over your head like
a black mirror or something.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:11.120
It's not it's not that at all.

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:15.520
In fact, that it was it was rolled out in
this place called Rong Chung in Shandong,

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:17.600
in northern China.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:19.990
And it was the first place that they they
rolled it out.

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:21.270
And it's no secret.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:25.709
In fact, I found the government documents
that are out there in public, and I translated

00:13:25.709 --> 00:13:26.709
them myself.

00:13:26.709 --> 00:13:28.649
And that's what I based my video on.

00:13:28.649 --> 00:13:34.079
And what it did was teach me how it works,
you know, from a test standpoint, testing

00:13:34.079 --> 00:13:35.970
out on the city and its people.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:40.480
It's mandatory there, but it's not necessarily
something that's used elsewhere.

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:41.480
Right.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:42.480
There are other cities.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.649
In fact, I reached out to a lot of people
around China to ask them if they even knew

00:13:45.649 --> 00:13:46.649
about it.

00:13:46.649 --> 00:13:49.100
I would say a good chunk of people hadn't
even heard about it.

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:51.269
But then there were there were cities, there
were other cities.

00:13:51.269 --> 00:13:55.360
And like in Chengdu and I talked to people
in NY, I talked to people all over China that

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:56.360
did know about it.

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.960
In fact, their city had some sort of version
of it.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:04.819
And what it is, it's really trying to reward
good behavior with a point based system and

00:14:04.819 --> 00:14:06.690
then punish bad behavior.

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:11.319
And unfortunately, when I went through those
documents and I spoke to people around China,

00:14:11.319 --> 00:14:17.139
it looks like it's trying to reward good societal
behavior, which is probably a fine it's a

00:14:17.139 --> 00:14:18.139
fine thing.

00:14:18.139 --> 00:14:21.529
It's like, you know, I don't have a criminal
record, so my score goes higher.

00:14:21.529 --> 00:14:22.529
Right.

00:14:22.529 --> 00:14:28.610
But unfortunately, a good proportion of those
crimes, of those things that lower your score

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:33.550
are things about speaking out against the
government, about posting messages of dissent

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:41.449
on on forums or on on chat groups, on, you
know, having weird religious beliefs, as they

00:14:41.449 --> 00:14:43.519
call it, you know, cult like religious beliefs.

00:14:43.519 --> 00:14:48.649
It turns out that a lot of it is aimed at
reducing personal freedom so that people don't

00:14:48.649 --> 00:14:50.220
dissent against the government.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:55.970
Speaker 1: Once you're sort of branded or
stigmatized or assigned, you know, a lesser

00:14:55.970 --> 00:15:00.649
status, whether it's specifically point based
or not, how do you recover from that?

00:15:00.649 --> 00:15:04.779
How can you how can you get back to sort of
being on everyone's good side, so to speak?

00:15:04.779 --> 00:15:09.240
Speaker 4: Well, unfortunately, if you're
on like the low that this is what I pointed

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:14.029
out in my in my video series is that when
I spoke to people, there's no even though

00:15:14.029 --> 00:15:18.751
there's a sliding scale of points, if the
government deems you the lowest score, you

00:15:18.751 --> 00:15:20.290
just get the lowest score.

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:23.079
There's not there's no rule of law, rhyme
or reason in this.

00:15:23.079 --> 00:15:27.250
I think people really rely on this western
lens of like everything works to a certain

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:28.250
standard.

00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:31.130
Everything works because of rules and laws
is not really how it works.

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:36.160
Like if China wants to throw you the lowest
score and make you like ostracized in society,

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:37.899
they'll just do that right off the bat.

00:15:37.899 --> 00:15:38.899
Right.

00:15:38.899 --> 00:15:41.350
Especially if it's something about the government
or the story in jail.

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:46.500
But if a person is, like labeled at the bottom,
I mean, your your kids probably aren't going

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:48.199
to get into schools.

00:15:48.199 --> 00:15:49.370
Right?

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:52.899
You know, you're not going to be able to get
employment opportunities at least above the

00:15:52.899 --> 00:15:57.580
lowest, you know, blue collar kind of construction,
construction type jobs, even if you're well-educated.

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:02.290
And to get out of that, if you're at the bottom,
you're not going anywhere.

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:04.920
But if you get out of that, you have to do
good deeds, right?

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:09.920
You have to participate in local government
activities.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:11.230
You have to.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:15.029
I mean, there are there are some clauses in
these documents that say if you rat someone

00:16:15.029 --> 00:16:18.870
out, basically that's doing bad things, then
you get to move up.

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:26.269
It's basically a really restrictive way of
keeping people in their spot.

00:16:26.269 --> 00:16:30.930
They don't really want people to go up or
down necessarily.

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:36.680
It's almost like there is no recourse for
people to exit once they've already been deemed

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:37.769
at that lower caste.

00:16:37.769 --> 00:16:41.540
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to ask about
in the couple of minutes we have left.

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:45.560
You weren't there during the COVID lockdowns,
but.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:50.759
It's been widely reported that, you know,
there are these videos of the vehicles pumping

00:16:50.759 --> 00:16:55.850
out disinfectant on the street, which is,
of course, of questionable or no actual preventive

00:16:55.850 --> 00:16:58.019
value for for COVID whatsoever.

00:16:58.019 --> 00:17:05.209
The COVID zero strategy of sort of close to
complete lockdown, etc., etc..

00:17:05.209 --> 00:17:10.199
Can you talk a little bit about whether any
of what you read about in the context of COVID

00:17:10.199 --> 00:17:14.089
was surprising based on your experiences when
you did live live there?

00:17:14.089 --> 00:17:20.270
Speaker 4: I know, honestly, like when I see
when I saw the heavy handed approach of, you

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:25.190
know, Xi Jinping's government, really not
just when he took over, but like later on

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:30.650
when he achieved full, full power within this
within the Chinese government, when he consolidated

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:34.809
that power through the anti-corruption drive
and things like this, I wasn't surprised to

00:17:34.809 --> 00:17:39.770
see what kind of methodology was coming out
with the Zero-Covid policy, because there's

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:44.809
a there's a thing within the Chinese government,
especially, especially true under the current

00:17:44.809 --> 00:17:47.900
leadership, is that they can't be wrong.

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:52.250
So when the Chinese government set out to
say, hey, look at the world failing with the

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:57.289
COVID response, look at we're going to be
the ones that kind of deal with it very harshly

00:17:57.289 --> 00:17:58.289
in the beginning.

00:17:58.289 --> 00:18:02.260
But we're going to show the world as kind
of as a brand how this authoritarian style

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:03.760
government actually works.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:04.760
Right.

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:06.270
We can take away people's personal freedoms
and stuff.

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:08.130
But isn't that better than dying?

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:10.690
Isn't that better than millions of people
dying?

00:18:10.690 --> 00:18:12.810
And honestly, it kind of worked in the beginning.

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:15.640
I think a lot of people are like, you know,
China's pretty brutal.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:18.390
But at the same time, you know, look at how
they've dealt with this.

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:20.780
A lot of people didn't die in comparison to
the rest of the world.

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:22.850
Speaker 1: That's, I guess, if you believe
the numbers.

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:23.850
Right.

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:24.850
I mean, there's also a question as to.

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:25.850
Yeah, but of course.

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:29.790
Speaker 4: Yeah, I'm I'm always speaking like
a normal reader.

00:18:29.790 --> 00:18:30.790
Yes.

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:31.790
Looking at the news.

00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:32.790
Right.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:33.790
Right.

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:34.790
Let's pretend like you believe the numbers.

00:18:34.790 --> 00:18:38.820
So then people are in a position to say, you
know what, this other style of governance,

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:41.429
maybe they're not going to jump on and be
like, yeah, China's the future, China's the

00:18:41.429 --> 00:18:42.429
way.

00:18:42.429 --> 00:18:43.620
But they'll look at it and say, You know what?

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:47.470
Maybe this kind of reporting that I see about
China is not really accurate.

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:48.470
Right.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:52.120
And what happens is, unfortunately, when you
get new variants, when you get something that

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:57.110
spreads so crazy like al-Muqrin and then the
rest of the world has found out ways to deal

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:02.760
with it, whether it be RNA vaccinations, things
that have been proven to work.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:07.570
China didn't adopt those things out of faith
to save face, to say that we have our own

00:19:07.570 --> 00:19:11.790
homegrown method, we have our own vaccine,
which is not very effective, by the way.

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:12.790
Right.

00:19:12.790 --> 00:19:13.790
Right.

00:19:13.790 --> 00:19:16.540
We have our own lockdown strategies and we've
already put in the work.

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:19.570
So we don't really have to suffer through
this anymore while you guys still have to.

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:21.700
And the rest of the world is kind of saying,
well, what do you mean?

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:25.289
Like it's not number one is not is the new
variants not as bad?

00:19:25.289 --> 00:19:27.470
Number two, a lot of our population is vaccinated.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:31.830
And number three, we have an actual dynamic
approach on how to deal with it that doesn't

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:34.770
necessarily restrict people's personal freedoms.

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:37.370
And China says, nope, nope, blinders on.

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:40.280
We have zero COVID, we have zero COVID, we
have zero COVID.

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:45.250
And that can't be proven wrong because in
the top down leadership in China, there is

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:48.880
no there is no saying that the government
is wrong about their strategy.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.909
They have to be right and see it through.

00:19:50.909 --> 00:19:55.179
And we're seeing where Xi Jinping is currently
trying to get into his third term.

00:19:55.179 --> 00:20:02.690
He has to make sure that his policies not
only worked, but nobody has any sort of dissent

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:04.590
or disapproval with them.

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:10.289
So we're looking at a situation now where
zero COVID is either going to change and rebrand

00:20:10.289 --> 00:20:16.130
to the to save leader leadership face or is
going to continue down this very draconian

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:17.130
route.

